fix(runner): retry transient ACP writes instead of killing the connection

The vendored acp-http-client's postMessage had no retry, and on ANY error it
called failReadable(), which sets closed=true and errors the readable stream.
A single transient `SocketError: other side closed` therefore tore down the
whole ACP transport rather than dropping one message, wedging the turn.

Patch postMessage (pnpm patch, same mechanism as the existing sandbox-agent
patch) to retry transient failures with bounded jittered backoff: 3 attempts at
~150/300/600ms. isRetryableAcpWriteError retries socket-level errors
(ECONNRESET, SocketError, "other side closed", fetch failed, UND_ERR_SOCKET,
including .cause-wrapped) plus 5xx/408/429; a 4xx AcpHttpError fails
immediately. failReadable now fires only once the budget is exhausted or on a
non-retryable error, so a blip that succeeds on retry no longer closes the
connection. Success semantics are unchanged.

